Description: Fix an out-of-bounds write when a file ends in an include with no newline
 At the end of the outermost file, the <<EOF>> rule decrements incl_buff to -1.
 If the last line has no trailing newline, it returns EOSTMT without restoring
 incl_buff, so an "include" statement on that line makes lex_include_file()
 write include_stack[-1] and line_num_stack[-1] (lexer.l).  Reproduced with
 UBSan: "index -1 out of bounds for type 'YY_BUFFER_STATE[15]'"; without
 sanitizers makedepf90 dies with SIGBUS or SIGSEGV:
   printf "module m\nend module m\ninclude 'inc.h'" > main.f90
   makedepf90 main.f90
 .
 Reset incl_buff to 0 before returning, as the other branch already does.
Author: Ash Pera <ashley.pera@noaa.gov>
Forwarded: no
Last-Updated: 2026-11-28
--- a/lexer.l
+++ b/lexer.l
@@ -155,6 +155,7 @@
                             yyterminate();
                         } else {
                             /* "EOF without \n first" cases. */
+                            incl_buff = 0;
                             RETURN(EOSTMT);
                         }
                     } else {
